# Postgres. Local: `supabase start` prints DB URL. Production: the provisioned instance.
DATABASE_URL=postgresql://user:pass@host:5432/db

# Absolute base URL used to build magic links and OAuth redirect targets.
NEXT_PUBLIC_APP_URL=http://localhost:3000

# Supabase, used as an identity provider for Google sign-in (lib/supabase.ts) —
# and, since 2026-09-20, as an OBJECT STORE for reel cover frames. Gaja issues
# its own session after sign-in, and still never reads a TABLE through Supabase:
# lib/db.ts remains the only way to Postgres.
#
# THE EXCEPTION, WRITTEN DOWN RATHER THAN SMUGGLED IN. This file used to say
# Supabase was an identity provider and nothing else. Storing objects is the
# first data-plane use of it in this codebase, and it was a deliberate choice:
# Instagram's thumbnail URLs carry an `oe=` expiry measured at ~4.5 days, so the
# only way to keep a reel's cover is to copy the bytes somewhere we own. See
# supabase/migrations/20260920000009_reel_thumbnails.sql and lib/storage.ts for
# the bucket, its public/private argument, and the boundary the exception stops
# at. If you find a Supabase client reading application rows, the exception has
# been abused.
#
# Deliberately not NEXT_PUBLIC_: nothing in the browser talks to Supabase. The
# browser does load IMAGES from the Storage host, which is a plain public URL and
# needs no key — next.config.ts allows exactly that one path prefix.
# Local: `supabase start` prints all three. Hosted: Dashboard -> Project Settings -> API.
# Optional. Left unset, the sign-in screen shows email only and hides the Google
# button entirely, rather than offering a route that dead-ends in an error.
SUPABASE_URL=
SUPABASE_ANON_KEY=

# SERVER-ONLY, and the strongest credential in this file after IG_SESSION_ID: the
# service role key BYPASSES RLS on every table and bucket in the project. It is
# used for exactly one thing — uploading a reel's cover frame into the
# `reel-thumbs` bucket (lib/storage.ts), because writing to Storage needs a role
# that the anon key does not have. Never NEXT_PUBLIC_ (that inlines it into the
# browser bundle at build time), never logged, never in a response body.
#
# Optional. Left unset, ingest still saves every reel and every venue it named —
# the pass just reports `thumbs_failed` and the deck falls back to the stock
# stills in lib/reel-thumb.ts. A missing cover must never cost a reel.
# Verify the whole round trip with ./scripts/verify-reel-thumbnail.sh.
SUPABASE_SERVICE_ROLE_KEY=

# Naver Maps, used by the "near you" map on home (app/(app)/home/nearby-map.tsx).
# Naver, not Google: the places are all in Seoul, addressed by Korean road
# address, and Google's Korean basemap has no driving directions, thin POI
# coverage and often no Korean venue name at all.
#
# The client ID is PUBLIC by design — it is compiled into the browser bundle and
# appears in the script URL on every page load. It cannot be made secret; restrict
# it instead, with a domain (service URL) whitelist on the key in the NCP console.
# Optional. Left unset, the map falls back to a plain list of the nearby places,
# so a fresh clone still gets a working screen.
NEXT_PUBLIC_NAVER_MAP_CLIENT_ID=

# SERVER-ONLY, and a real secret. Deliberately not NEXT_PUBLIC_: the JS Maps API
# does not use it at all. It authenticates the server-side Geocoding and
# Directions APIs, so it must never reach a client component or the bundle.
#
# Required by lib/research/geocode.ts, which throws GeocoderNotConfiguredError by
# name when it is unset. Geocoding sends BOTH this and the public client id above
# — `x-ncp-apigw-api-key` and `x-ncp-apigw-api-key-id` — so the pair must come
# from the SAME NCP application, and that application must have the Geocoding
# product enabled. A key whose application lacks it loads the map perfectly and
# answers 401 here, which looks like a wrong secret and is not.
# Verify both with ./scripts/verify-geocode.sh.
NAVER_MAP_CLIENT_SECRET=

# NEXT_PUBLIC_GOOGLE_MAPS_API_KEY is no longer used — Naver replaced it. Delete it
# from .env.local and from the Vercel project.

# OPEN: no transactional-email provider is chosen yet. Magic links are written to the
# server console in development and lib/mail.ts throws in production until one exists.

# Gemini, used by the reel caption extractor (lib/extract/caption.ts) — rung one
# of the extraction ladder, which reads venues out of a shared reel's caption.
#
# SERVER-ONLY, and a real, billed secret. Deliberately not NEXT_PUBLIC_: that
# prefix inlines the value into the browser bundle at build time, and this key
# is never needed in a browser. Nothing under app/ may import it.
# Get one at https://aistudio.google.com/apikey.
# Required by lib/extract/caption.ts, which throws by name when it is unset.
# The model-free fallback (lib/extract/caption-grammar.ts) needs no key at all.
#
# The SAME key also pays for rung two (lib/extract/asr.ts), which hands a shared
# reel's mp4 to the same model and gets back both the spoken audio and the text
# burned into the frames in one call. That rung runs only when the caption did
# not answer — see the boundary table in lib/extract/confidence.ts — so an
# ordinary listicle never reaches it. It throws AsrNotConfiguredError by name
# when this is unset.
# Verify both rungs and both upload transports with ./scripts/verify-reel-asr.sh.
GEMINI_API_KEY=

# Also required by the in-app assistant (lib/agent/run.ts) — the floating button
# above the tab bar. That one uses `gemini-3.1-flash` rather than flash-lite: it
# has to choose between tools and read blog prose, not copy fields out of a
# caption. Unset, the assistant answers with "지금은 대답할 수 없어요" and logs the
# reason server-side; it never falls back to answering without the tools.

# ─── Apify ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Naver blog review text, used by lib/research/sources/naver-blog.ts and reached
# by the assistant's `research_place_reviews` tool. The actor is
# `maximedupre/naver-blog-review-scraper`; the wait-digest spec §1 chose it over
# Naver's official Search API because that API returns ~200-character snippets
# and a wait claim's day/time qualifier is routinely further from the number than
# that.
#
# SERVER-ONLY, and a real, billed secret — Apify charges per actor run (~$0.16/day
# at the spec's 500-place refresh volume). Never prefix it NEXT_PUBLIC_, never log
# it, never put it in a response body. Get one at https://console.apify.com/settings/integrations.
#
# Optional. Left unset, `apifyConfigured()` is false, the research tool returns a
# "not configured" result, and the assistant is instructed to say it could not
# check the reviews rather than answering from the model's own memory as though
# it had. Everything else — saved places, nearby, course building — still works.
APIFY_TOKEN=

# Override only if the actor is renamed or forked. Defaults to the actor above,
# so an unset value is not a failure. Slash form, not Apify's `~` URL form.
APIFY_NAVER_BLOG_ACTOR=

# ─── Instagram DM inbox poller ───────────────────────────────────────────────
# lib/ingest/inbox/instagram-poll.ts reads shared reels out of a logged-in
# Instagram account's DMs by calling GET /api/v1/direct_v2/inbox/ with that
# account's own session cookies. This is NOT a supported Instagram interface and
# it carries real ban risk; see docs/gaja/reel-extraction-findings.md. It is a
# stopgap until the Meta Messaging API webhook replaces it, at which point all
# three of these variables are deleted.
#
# ALL THREE ARE SERVER-ONLY SECRETS, and `IG_SESSION_ID` is the strongest
# credential in this file: it is a bearer token for the entire Instagram account
# — not scoped, not read-only. Anyone holding it IS the account. Never prefix any
# of them with NEXT_PUBLIC_ (that inlines the value into the browser bundle at
# build time), never log them, never put them in a response body.
#
# HOW TO CAPTURE THEM. `sessionid` is httpOnly, so no script can read it and
# `document.cookie` will not show it. Sign in to instagram.com in a browser, then
# DevTools → Application → Cookies → https://www.instagram.com, and copy the
# values by hand:
#   IG_SESSION_ID   ← the `sessionid` cookie
#   IG_CSRF_TOKEN   ← the `csrftoken` cookie
#   IG_DS_USER_ID   ← the `ds_user_id` cookie (the account's own numeric id; the
#                     poller uses it to skip the account's own outgoing shares)
#
# THESE EXPIRE. A session ends on logout, on a password change, when Instagram
# challenges the account, and on its own after some weeks — and there is no
# refresh. When it does, the poller sees a 401 or a challenge, TRIPS ITS CIRCUIT
# BREAKER, and refuses to poll again until a person clears the challenge in the
# Instagram app, re-captures the cookies above, and then resets the breaker by
# hand:
#   update ingest_state
#      set breaker_tripped_at = null, breaker_reason = null
#    where source = 'instagram-web-inbox-poll';
# The refusal is deliberate and must not be automated. Retrying into a challenge
# is how the account gets banned.
IG_SESSION_ID=
IG_CSRF_TOKEN=
IG_DS_USER_ID=

# Shared secret guarding POST/GET /api/internal/ingest/instagram, the endpoint
# that runs one ingest pass. Vercel Cron sends it as `Authorization: Bearer
# <value>` on every invocation; the route compares it with crypto.timingSafeEqual
# and refuses to run at all when this is unset — an unset secret is a published
# ingest trigger, not an open door worth leaving open.
#
# Generate with: openssl rand -base64 48
#
# PLAN CONSTRAINT. vercel.ts schedules this cron at '0 3 * * *' (once daily)
# because Vercel's Hobby plan only permits once-per-day cron jobs; per-minute and
# every-N-minutes schedules require Pro. Daily is far too slow to be the real
# cadence — on Pro, change that one line to '*/20 * * * *'. Until then (and this
# project has no Vercel deployment at all yet — org admin approval is pending)
# the route is driven by hand, which works identically:
#   curl -sS -H "Authorization: Bearer $CRON_SECRET" \
#        http://localhost:3000/api/internal/ingest/instagram
# The poller enforces its own 15-minute floor with jitter regardless of who calls
# it, so an over-eager caller gets `skipped: "min-interval"` rather than traffic
# to Instagram.
CRON_SECRET=
